1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,320
why the British starved the Indians, explained like you are five.

2
00:00:03,320 --> 00:00:07,400
Step one, 1858, Britain takes direct control of India.

3
00:00:07,400 --> 00:00:10,480
300 million people, the richest subcontinent on earth,

4
00:00:10,480 --> 00:00:14,840
spices, cotton, opium, tea, textiles, gold, Britain extracts it all.

5
00:00:14,840 --> 00:00:19,920
Historians estimate Britain drains $45 trillion from India over 200 years,

6
00:00:19,920 --> 00:00:22,320
the most profitable colony in human history.

7
00:00:22,320 --> 00:00:24,360
India feeds Britain's industrial revolution.

8
00:00:24,360 --> 00:00:25,760
India fights Britain's wars.

9
00:00:25,760 --> 00:00:29,400
India builds Britain's railways with Indian labor and Indian money.

10
00:00:29,480 --> 00:00:33,720
And pays for the privilege through a tax system designed to transfer wealth upward

11
00:00:33,720 --> 00:00:37,120
to British landlords, British administrators, British shareholders,

12
00:00:37,120 --> 00:00:40,840
the people growing the food taxed so heavily they cannot afford to eat it.

13
00:00:40,840 --> 00:00:43,560
The system works perfectly until the rains stop.

14
00:00:43,560 --> 00:00:47,360
Step two, 1876, the monsoon fails across southern India.

15
00:00:47,360 --> 00:00:49,760
Crops wither, wells dry up, people begin to starve.

16
00:00:49,760 --> 00:00:52,240
The British response is immediate and precise.

17
00:00:52,240 --> 00:00:53,760
They do almost nothing.

18
00:00:53,760 --> 00:00:56,960
The Viceroy Lord Lytton is a follower of free market economics.

19
00:00:56,960 --> 00:01:00,080
He believes government intervention in food markets distorts prices.

20
00:01:00,080 --> 00:01:02,920
So while Indians starve, grain sits in warehouses.

21
00:01:02,920 --> 00:01:06,680
While Indians starve, food is loaded onto ships and exported to Britain,

22
00:01:06,680 --> 00:01:10,600
not because Britain needs it, because the market says it is more profitable there.

23
00:01:10,600 --> 00:01:16,640
In 1877, at the height of the famine, Lord Lytton hosts a banquet for 68,000 people

24
00:01:16,640 --> 00:01:19,840
to celebrate Queen Victoria being declared Empress of India.

25
00:01:19,840 --> 00:01:21,160
The banquet lasts one week.

26
00:01:21,160 --> 00:01:22,840
Outside, bodies in the streets.

27
00:01:22,840 --> 00:01:27,400
Step three, the famine of 1876 kills 5.5 million people.

28
00:01:27,400 --> 00:01:31,640
Before India recovers, another famine, 1896, then again 1899.

29
00:01:31,640 --> 00:01:35,360
Three catastrophic famines in 25 years, 29 million dead in total.

30
00:01:35,360 --> 00:01:39,680
Each time, the same pattern, food available, food exported, people die.

31
00:01:39,680 --> 00:01:43,320
One British official, Sir Richard Temple, tries to set up famine relief camps.

32
00:01:43,320 --> 00:01:47,200
He is removed from his position, criticized for being too generous with Indian lives.

33
00:01:47,200 --> 00:01:51,120
His replacement cuts rations below what the Nazis later feed concentration camp

34
00:01:51,120 --> 00:01:52,760
prisoners and introduces a rule.

35
00:01:52,760 --> 00:01:57,280
Anyone who walks more than 10 miles to reach a relief camp is too healthy to need relief

36
00:01:57,280 --> 00:01:58,520
and is turned away.

37
00:01:58,520 --> 00:02:00,080
The camps become death camps.

38
00:02:00,080 --> 00:02:02,320
The official name, the famine code.

39
00:02:02,320 --> 00:02:06,920
Step four, Winston Churchill becomes Secretary of State for India in 1940.

40
00:02:06,920 --> 00:02:08,440
Another famine strikes Bengal.

41
00:02:08,440 --> 00:02:09,840
Three million people die.

42
00:02:09,840 --> 00:02:14,560
Churchill diverts food shipments away from India to British troops and European stockpiles.

43
00:02:14,560 --> 00:02:17,680
When told of the mass deaths, he asks why Gandhi hasn't died yet.

44
00:02:17,680 --> 00:02:21,480
He writes in a memo that Indians are a beastly people with a beastly religion

45
00:02:21,480 --> 00:02:25,000
who brought the famine on themselves by breeding like rabbits.

46
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:28,160
After independence, India never has a famine again, not one.

47
00:02:28,160 --> 00:02:30,880
In 75 years, the famines were not caused by drought.

48
00:02:30,880 --> 00:02:32,680
They were not caused by overpopulation.

49
00:02:32,680 --> 00:02:36,680
They were caused by policy, deliberate, documented, defended at the time.

50
00:02:36,680 --> 00:02:39,880
29 million people in the richest colony the world had ever seen,

51
00:02:39,880 --> 00:02:41,800
starved to death by the people running it.

52
00:02:41,800 --> 00:02:44,160
And that's just one story inside Paladin.

53
00:02:44,160 --> 00:02:46,560
It's like Duolingo, but for history lovers.

54
00:02:46,560 --> 00:02:49,840
Download it now and become the most intelligent person in the room.

